    {
      "id": 40,
      "type": "text",
      "title": "Reading this dashboard",
      "gridPos": { "x": 0, "y": 41, "w": 24, "h": 9 },
      "options": {
        "mode": "markdown",
        "content": "### Setup\n\nSet the **Metric prefix** variable to match your nebula `stats` config. The Prometheus exporter builds names as `namespace_subsystem_metric` with `.` replaced by `_`, so an empty `stats.namespace` plus `stats.subsystem: nebula` gives `nebula`.\n\nnebula's counters are go-metrics counters exported as Prometheus **gauges**. They are monotonic within a process and reset to zero on restart, so `rate()` is still the correct function.\n\n### What is measured vs derived\n\n| Panel | Source |\n|---|---|\n| Relayed / direct tunnel counts | Measured — `hostmap.main.tunnels.*` |\n| Relay inventory by type and state | Measured — `hostmap.main.relays.*` |\n| Promotion attempts, probes, re-queries, successes | Measured — `hostmap.promotion.*` |\n| **Est. time on a relay** | **Derived** — Little's Law, see below |\n\n### The dwell-time estimate\n\nNebula does not time how long a tunnel stays relayed. `relayed_tunnels / rate(relay_to_direct)` estimates the mean, and is an **upper bound**: relayed tunnels also exit by being torn down, and that exit path is not in the denominator. Treat it as an order of magnitude, and read ∞ as \"nothing is promoting right now\".\n\nMeasuring this properly needs per-tunnel dwell instrumentation that does not exist yet.\n\n### Why a tunnel gets stuck\n\n`TryPromoteBest` is gated on a **packet counter**, not a timer — `counters.try_promote` (1000) for probes and `counters.requery_every_packets` (5000, min `timers.requery_wait_duration` apart) for the lighthouse re-query. Only the re-query triggers a fresh `HostPunchNotification` on the peer, so it is the one that can actually open a NAT hole. A low-traffic tunnel accumulates those counters slowly and can sit on a relay indefinitely.\n\nLevers, roughly in order of value:\n\n1. `punchy.delay` lower (e.g. `200ms`) — improves which path wins the initial race, so fewer tunnels need promoting at all.\n2. `punchy.target_all_remotes: true` — keeps holes open on relayed tunnels, so promotion probes land instead of hitting a closed NAT.\n3. `counters.requery_every_packets` / `timers.requery_wait_duration` lower — fires the punch-triggering re-query sooner.\n4. `punchy.respond: true` on hard-NAT nodes — lets the reverse direction establish."
      }
    }
  ]
